0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","$L2",null,{"study":{"id":"stuxnet","title":"When code reached the physical world","category":"Cyber-physical case","year":"2010","subtitle":"Stuxnet and industrial control systems","fictional":false,"difficulty":"Advanced concepts","summary":"A sophisticated worm targeted industrial control environments, demonstrating how cyber operations could produce physical effects.","story":["Stuxnet attracted attention because it affected industrial control systems rather than merely stealing documents or defacing websites. Public technical reporting examined its propagation, targeting, and interaction with Siemens control environments.","The case illustrates the importance of asset inventory, segmentation, trusted engineering workflows, removable-media controls, and monitoring of process behavior. A normal-looking IT event can have operational consequences when systems are connected.","Attribution and the legal characterization of the operation have been debated in public sources. A classroom analysis should distinguish documented technical behavior from contested claims about sponsorship, intent, and international-law thresholds. The case is not a lab instruction to interact with industrial systems."],"timeline":[{"date":"2010","event":"Public discovery and technical analysis of Stuxnet."},{"date":"2010–2011","event":"Industrial-security advisories and mitigation guidance develop."},{"date":"Later research","event":"Technical and policy analysis continues; attribution claims remain distinct from proof in a court."}],"issue":"How cyber operations can affect industrial processes and why safety, evidence, and response must be coordinated.","holding":"Technical lesson: cyber-physical investigation requires process context, controlled safety measures, and careful separation of technical findings from disputed attribution.","lessons":["Map IT and operational-technology dependencies.","Do not treat all system failures as malware or all malware as terrorism.","Preserve engineering and process context.","Coordinate safety and evidence decisions.","Use qualified language for attribution and legal thresholds."],"questions":["What additional evidence is needed in a cyber-physical incident?","Why could an immediate rebuild be unsafe or destroy evidence?","How do you separate technical observation from a political claim?"],"sources":["stuxnet","nist-csf"],"topics":["5.4","5.5","5.6"]}}],[["$","script","script-0",{"src":"/_next/static/chunks/07qngb6s2_y2q.js","async":true}]],["$","$L3",null,{"children":["$","$4",null,{"name":"Next.MetadataOutlet","children":"$@5"}]}]]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
